# =============================================================================
# ≈ — tolerance equality wants numbers, not text
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • `a ≈ b` holds when the pair is inside a relative tolerance, so the
#     operator's admission law is the numeric one: both operands must be
#     numbers of the same family.
#   • Text has no tolerance row — two cords are either `≡` or not. The pair is
#     refused at check time instead of failing later with no MIR primitive.
#   • `≉` follows the same law; it is the negation of the same numeric rule.
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • Comparing two strings with `≈` and expecting a "close enough" text
#     match; use `≡` for whole-value equality.
#   • Writing string literals here and expecting a different answer — the
#     refusal is about the carriers, not about the values.
#
# See also: ≡, ≅, textus
# =============================================================================

# operatores/approx-string-reject — tolerance equality over two cords
#
# This exemplum intentionally fails to check: `≈` has no text row, so the
# checker rejects the same-typed non-numeric pair rather than admitting it and
# deferring the failure to MIR validation.
#
# The operands are bound names, not literals, so no constant-fold path is
# involved: the rejection is the checker's admission law for `≈`.

main {
    let string a ← "ab"
    let string b ← "ac"
    print a ≈ b
}
